/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Deflection grows with time: the immediate (short-term) sag under load is followed by creep of the concrete under the sustained load and by shrinkage as it dries, which together can double or triple it.
   - L/350 equals 20 mm at a span of 7 m, so the 20 mm cap governs only for spans longer than 7 m.
   - For spans up to 10 m, a beam that satisfies the span/depth rule is taken to meet both limits without computing deflection:
   - (L/d)basic = 7 for a cantilever, 20 for a simply supported span and 26 for a continuous span; kt is the modification factor for tension steel (IS 456 Fig. 4), kc for compression steel (Fig. 5) and kf for flanged beams (Fig. 6).
   - kt depends on the steel percentage pt and on the service stress in the steel, fs = 0.58 fy × (Ast required/Ast provided).
   - If the check fails, more depth helps most, because L/d and fs fall together; extra tension steel or compression steel also helps.
   - Cracks in a beam cannot be avoided, because concrete cracks at a tensile stress of only 0.7√fck.
   - IS 456 Annex F. acr is the distance from the point considered to the surface of the nearest bar; Cmin the clear cover to the main bars; x the depth of the cracked neutral axis; a the depth of the point considered (a = D at the soffit); ε1 t
*/
