/* book Worked example: Q: A roof beam 250 mm wide and 450 mm deep (d = 400 mm) spans 9 m, simply supported, and carries Mu = 125 kN·m. M25, Fe415. The design needs Ast = 1048 mm²; 4 bars of 20 mm (1257 mm²) are provided. Check deflection by the span/depth rule, and if it fails, test 2 bars of 16 mm at the top and a 500 mm deep beam (d = 450 mm, Ast required 885 mm²).
   1. Actual L/d = 9000/400 = 22.5. The span is under 10 m, so (L/d)basic = 20.
   2. pt = 100 × 1257/(250 × 400) = 1.26 %. fs = 0.58 × 415 × 1048/1257 = 200.8 N/mm².
   3. From Fig. 4 (or the fit): kt = 1/[0.225 + 0.00322 × 200.8 + 0.625 log₁₀ 1.26] = 1/0.934 = 1.07.
   4. Allowed L/d = 20 × 1.07 = 21.4 < 22.5: the beam fails the deflection check.
   5. Option 1, 2 bars of 16 mm at the top (402 mm²): pc = 100 × 402/(250 × 400) = 0.40 %, kc = 1 + 0.40/3.40 = 1.12. Allowed L/d = 20 × 1.07 × 1.12 = 24.0 > 22.5: passes.
   6. Option 2, D = 500 mm: L/d = 9000/450 = 20.0; pt = 100 × 1257/(250 × 450) = 1.12 %, fs = 0.58 × 415 × 885/1257 = 169.6 N/mm², kt = 1.25. Allowed 20 × 1.25 = 25.0 > 20.0: passes with margin.
   Answer: With d = 400 mm the beam fails (22.5 against 21.4); 2 bars of 16 mm at the top bring the limit to 24.0, and a 500 mm deep beam to 25.0 against 20.0. */
