/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Steel and concrete at the same level have the same strain, so the steel stress is m times the concrete stress there.
   - Before cracking, the whole concrete section b × D acts, and the steel adds (m − 1)Ast: m Ast carries the steel's force, minus the Ast of concrete the bars already displace.
   - b, D and d in mm; Ast in mm²; I in mm⁴.
   - The first flexural crack opens when the bottom fibre reaches the flexural tensile strength fcr = 0.7√fck (IS 456 clause 6.2.2).
   - Mcr in N·mm.
   - In a normally designed beam Mcr is well below the service moment, so at service load the beam is cracked over most of its span.
   - Above Mcr the concrete below the neutral axis is ignored and the steel becomes m Ast.
   - fc is the concrete stress at the top fibre and fs the steel stress, both in N/mm², under the service moment M in N·mm.
   - The real stiffness lies between Icr and Igr, because the concrete between cracks still carries some tension.
   - M is the largest service moment; bw/b = 1 for a rectangle.
*/
